{
	title: 'Ctrl-F: one find dialog with filters and live results',
	spec: `Ctrl-F (terminal and web) opens one dialog laid out like Ctrl-O's (task v8y): the line "Find anything: text, tab, card, project, whatever", a blank row, the query field flush with the left edge (no label), a blank row, a live status line, a blank row, one row of filter toggles [x] user/assistant  [x] thinking  [x] tools  [x] other (all on by default), a blank row, then the results. With an empty query the results are the 50 most recently updated sessions (history file modified time) and the status says "Showing recent tabs"; once a query is typed it says "Searching…", "Showing 12 results, scanning 1,204 histories…" while tiers stream in, then "Showing 123 results" or "No results"; a failure says "Find failed: <error>" in the error color. Results update as tiers stream in without the list jumping under the cursor (new lower tiers append; a better hit in an already-shown tier inserts above only while the selection has not moved).

Keys: Down moves field, filters, results; Up goes back (from the first result to the filters). On the filters Left/Right move among them and Space, Enter or a click toggles one; the focused filter is lit like a picker selection. In the results Up/Down move the selection. Tab cycles field, each filter, results. Enter in the field or results opens the session tab at the selected result's block (the web scrolls it into view; the terminal tells where it is, as /go does, task 4qh); Escape closes. The footer names the focused part's keys: field "↓: filters · enter: open · esc: close", filters "←→: move · space: toggle · ↑↓: field/results · esc: close", results "↑↓: move · enter: open · esc: close". On the web the DOM focus follows. Typing restarts the query after a short debounce (about 80 ms). Each result row starts with the tab number of its open tab (right-aligned; blank for a closed session, whose id then follows its name), then the session name, age, kind marker (none for session matches) and snippet with the match highlighted; recent sessions show their working directory. The toggles are remembered for the next Ctrl-F. On the web it is an overlay that scrolls itself (tasks/README.md web rules), phone-sized touch targets. Not a question form in history: find is transient UI and leaves nothing in the session.
The dialog uses the shared transient modal and model-picker search field; Ctrl-F is reserved in the shared command list and advertised by shared key help, not the prompt's editing keys. Query/filter changes clear stale results immediately and cancel the preceding host request; Escape and replacing the dialog cancel pending debounce/search. Late batches cannot revive a dismissed query. Focus changes do not lose a pending debounced query. Results use v8's tier-replacement batches and final summary without duplicating rows; after explicit Up/Down selection, existing rows remain in place and only unseen hits append. Highlight all matching word substrings, preserving snippet context. Filters live only in the client, initially all enabled and retained across dialog openings; query, cursor and results reset on reopening. The prompt draft is untouched by searching.
Result block navigation uses v8's stable block ids and hrefs. Browser result rows are native links (including modifier-click/new-tab behavior), and direct session/block URLs reopen closed sessions without falling back to the first open tab or discarding the block hash. Invalid session addresses fall back to an available tab. Phone filters may wrap rather than overflow; toggle and result targets are at least 44px, with visible focus, labeled query, filter pressed state, selection state and live status. The overlay list owns scrolling, remains inside the dynamic viewport and keeps the selected result visible.
`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'v8 exposes request/kinds on find, find-cancel without an id, and per-tier replacement batches (not deltas); final done repeats a summary. Shared find-dialog + controller adapt this to existing modal machinery, with thin client adapters. Native result URLs required web startup to resume an explicitly addressed closed session before choosing a fallback tab.',
		'Manually checked real headless Chrome through CDP at 390x800 and 1280x800: screenshots inspected, 52 streamed rows with highlighted matches, 44px filters, bounded overlay/list scrolling without horizontal overflow; toggle survives Escape/reopen, Enter scrolls to #6, and a fresh URL reopens a closed session. Focus change during debounce and terminal off-tail block visibility covered by behavior tests.',
		'Bug (11 Oct): a find error (multi-line, from the host) was put in the bottom border hint; its newlines broke the terminal frame, smearing the dialog and repeating the Query row. Invariant: errors show complete in the modal error area (terminal wraps it below the list; web pre-wraps it), and modal border text is always one line (newlines become spaces).',
		'Bug (15 Oct): snippets started 45 chars before the match, so terminal rows clipped it away and showed no highlight. Invariant: a snippet has at most ~20 chars of lead, from a word start, padded so the match starts at the same column in every row (highlights line up). Rows are aligned columns (user request): tab number, session id, name cut to 24 chars so the text gets the room, age, kind, snippet. Find grows with the screen: terminal up to 180 columns (other modals keep 100), web min(90vw, 110rem).',
		'The responsive layout supersedes the fixed 24-character title and dialog caps above. Find uses nearly the full viewport (terminal: two columns at each side and one row above/below; web: 1rem margins). Both clients pass their measured row width to shared row formatting: reserve about 60 columns for content before giving remaining space to titles, up to the longest title. When space is tight omit the title before sacrificing content. Snippet lead shrinks with available content width, retaining aligned highlighted matches. Web measures character width and list width with a ResizeObserver; terminal recalculates from the frame width on resize. One-off checks at row widths 50, 70, 90, 120 and 180 verified match visibility/alignment, full titles at 180, and terminal frame rendering. Focused find/modal tests and typecheck passed; no browser visual check in this change.'
	],
	needs: ['v8', 'w4']
}
